= Expense Submission & Documentation

== Submission Timelines and Deadlines

Prompt reporting of business expenses is essential for timely financial accounting and accurate tax reporting.

#policy-rule("POL-DOC-001", "30-Day Submission Deadline")[
  All expense reports must be submitted within thirty (30) calendar days of the transaction date or the conclusion of the business trip, whichever occurs later. Expense claims submitted after sixty (60) days require written Vice President approval. Claims older than ninety (90) days are forfeited and strictly non-reimbursable.
]

== Mandatory Itemized Receipts

Every expense claim must be substantiated with valid, original proof of payment.

#policy-rule("POL-DOC-002", "Itemized Receipt Requirement")[
  Every expense exceeding €10.00 / \$10.00 must be substantiated with a detailed, itemized receipt or invoice. Summary credit card charge slips (showing only the grand total without line items) are insufficient and will result in claim rejection or partial deduction.
]

The receipt must clearly indicate:
- Merchant/vendor legal name and address
- Date and time of transaction
- Itemized list of goods or services purchased, including quantities and individual unit prices
- Taxes applied (VAT / Sales Tax)
- Total amount paid and currency

== Lost or Unavailable Receipts

In extraordinary circumstances where an original receipt cannot be retrieved, the employee must complete a Lost Receipt Declaration form.

#policy-warning("POL-DOC-003", "Lost Receipt Declaration Limit")[
  A Lost Receipt Declaration may be used for expenses not exceeding €50.00 / \$50.00, limited to a maximum of two (2) instances per calendar year per employee. Airfare, hotel folios, and rental car agreements cannot be claimed via a Lost Receipt Declaration under any circumstance.
]
